{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788269459,"nonce":"sTBl9JAxL0zxRSUYTzUfDY1F13s=","message":"VERIFIED, AND THE DIAGNOSIS IS HALF THE DEFECT. Verify-first pass of the round\nthat makes blueprints buildable, 2026-09-01, against `baseviz/render.py` and the\ndecompiled `Verse/GenAdj`.\n\n\n`px()` is unambiguous — `baseviz/render.py`:\n\n    def px(cx, cz):\n        \"\"\"Cell (map x, map z) -\u003e top-left pixel. The ONE place z flips to y.\"\"\"\n        return gx0 + (cx - ox) * scale, gy0 + (oz + h - 1 - cz) * scale\n\nso `x, y = px(gxc, gzc)` is the top-left pixel of the ONE cell at the building's\n`Position`, and `map-dump` publishes `at = Thing.Position`\n(`MapDumpVerbs.cs`, beside `[\"rot\"] = best.Rotation.ToStringHuman()`).\n`Verse/GenAdj.OccupiedRect(center, rot, size)` is\n`new CellRect(center.x - (size.x-1)/2, center.z - (size.z-1)/2, size.x, size.z)`\nafter `AdjustForRotation`, so for odd sizes `Position` IS the middle cell.\n`cx = x + (sw * scale) // 2 - tw // 2` therefore adds `sw/2` cells where it\nshould add `1/2`, and a 3-wide building's code lands one cell east. CONFIRMED.\n\n\n    cy = y + scale // 2 - ((sh - 1) * scale) // 2 - th // 2\n\nFor `sh = 1` the subtraction is zero and this is correct — which is the whole\nreason nobody has seen it, because **the specimen the issue was filed against is\na 3x1 `ElectricStove`**. Its height is 1. For `sh = 3` the term subtracts one\nfull cell, and since y increases southward the label lands **one cell NORTH of\ncentre**: the same magnitude of error as the x axis, the opposite direction, on\nthe same building.\n\nSo the two axes use two different and mutually inconsistent conventions, and at\nmost one of them could ever have been right. A fix that touches only `cx`, which\nis what this issue as written asks for, leaves every 1x3 and 3x3 building\nmislabelled — and leaves it mislabelled in a way the issue has now certified as\nchecked.\n\n\nDerived from `OccupiedRect` and verified case by case. Let `x, y = px(gxc, gzc)`\nand let `sw, sh` be the ROTATED dimensions.\n\n    minX  = gxc - (sw - 1) // 2                  # OccupiedRect's own arithmetic\n    maxZ  = gzc - (sh - 1) // 2 + sh - 1         # the north row\n    cx    = x - ((sw - 1) // 2) * scale + (sw * scale) // 2 - tw // 2\n    cy    = y - (maxZ - gzc)     * scale + (sh * scale) // 2 - th // 2\n\nChecks, where `s` = scale and the target is the geometric centre of the\nfootprint:\n\n| size | current cx | correct cx | current cy | correct cy |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | x + 0.5s | x + 0.5s | y + 0.5s | y + 0.5s |\n| 2 | x + 1.0s | x + 1.0s | y + 0.5s | y + 1.0s |\n| 3 | x + 1.5s | x + 0.5s | y - 0.5s | y + 0.5s |\n| 4 | x + 2.0s | x + 1.0s | y - 0.5s | y + 1.0s |\n\n**The even answer the acceptance asks for, stated:** an even-width building has\nno middle cell, so its centre is the BOUNDARY between the two middle cells —\n`x + (sw/2)*scale` exactly, i.e. a whole number of cells from the west edge.\nThat falls out of the formula above; it does not need a special case. Note the\ncurrent `cx` is accidentally correct for even widths and wrong for odd ones,\nwhich is presumably how the `size/2` got written.\n\n\n    if lab.get(\"rot\") in (\"East\", \"West\"):\n        sw, sh = sh, sw\n\nThis reproduces only HALF of `GenAdj.AdjustForRotation`. The method swaps\n`size.x`/`size.z` when `rot.IsHorizontal` — and then applies a centre shift\n**per axis, only when that axis's size is even**:\n\n    if (size.x % 2 == 0) { center.x += num; }\n    if (size.z % 2 == 0) { center.z += num2; }\n\n(with an early `return` when `size` is 1x1). So for an EVEN-sized rotated\nbuilding the renderer's derived footprint is displaced from the game's by one\ncell, and no amount of fixing `cx`/`cy` corrects it, because the rect itself is\nwrong. This is the same arithmetic family as `c718e4a`'s corner/centre finding —\nthe survey issue exists because the identical shift bit the mod on the placement\nside.\n\nCheapest correct fix: have the renderer derive the footprint the way the game\ndoes rather than by swapping, or have `map-dump` publish the occupied rect\nalongside `at` (it already holds the `Thing` and can call `OccupiedRect()`\ndirectly). The second is strictly better — it removes the arithmetic from the\nconsumer entirely, and it is the same \"publish the game's answer, never ask a\ncaller to derive it\" rule `c718e4a` pinned for `pos`.\n\n\nThe three existing bullets stand. Add:\n\n- An ODD-HEIGHT building (1x3 or 3x3) renders its code on its true centre cell.\n  This is the bullet the original specimen could not have caught.\n- An even-sized building with `rot` East or West renders its code inside its\n  actual occupied rect, and the rect the renderer derives equals\n  `GenAdj.OccupiedRect(at, rot, size)`.","files":null}]}